1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:08,717
This is my release workspace in Sheprd. I'd like a reminder down here in the status bar, where I can see it while I'm working.

2
00:00:08,717 --> 00:00:18,357
Moonframe is the toolkit behind this window. It lets you change the interface while the app is running, all the way from a label to a little app of your own.

3
00:00:18,367 --> 00:00:29,367
The window is native. Rust runs the application and its terminal and chat views. The layout around those views is written in Luau, a language in the Lua family.

4
00:00:29,367 --> 00:00:41,167
The sidebar, the activity icons and the status bar each have a layout you can edit. You can move existing controls, add a widget, or give a new tool its own place in the window.

5
00:00:41,167 --> 00:00:50,883
Let's make that reminder. The sparkles just above Settings open Make it yours. This is where you talk to an agent about the interface you're looking at.

6
00:00:50,883 --> 00:01:01,317
I'll choose Codex and describe the change in plain English. Add a small amber Release day label to the left of my status bar. Keep the existing items.

7
00:01:01,317 --> 00:01:11,597
Under Feedback and fixes, you can follow the agent as it reads the built-in layout and edits your personal copy. In this build, its final check reports an error.

8
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:22,280
Let's look at the window itself. There's Release day, in amber, alongside the usual status items. My terminal still shows the four tests I ran before the edit.

9
00:01:22,283 --> 00:01:32,317
Here's a bigger example. This layout adds a release checklist, a counter in the status bar, and an icon in the activity bar that opens the checklist.

10
00:01:32,317 --> 00:01:42,277
I'll open it and check off the first task. Watch the counter at the bottom change from zero of three to one of three. Both controls share the same state.

11
00:01:42,283 --> 00:01:51,363
Back to workspace brings me straight to my terminal. To change something in your own window, start with the sparkles and tell the agent what you'd like to see.
